  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The angry chef screams at his lazy kitchen staff for leaving a dirty plate. He orders everyone to clean the kitchen immediately while they secretly plot to calm him down with a special spiked drink.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что за инвалиды?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой идиот тарелку здесь оставил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне что, за всем следить надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас есть полчаса, чтобы всё убрать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Совсем крыша у него поехала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Надо ему срочно дать выпить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да как? Тут же камеры кругом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, кто там ещё пришёл?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, хотите выпить чайку?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой к чёрту чай?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли вон, я вам сказал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, ну попробуйте этот чай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, вам обязательно понравится.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что за инвалиды?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a bunch of idiots?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'oh' with 'disabled person'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой идиот тарелку здесь оставил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Which idiot left a plate here?' Chef Viktor links 'what a' directly with 'idiot' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне что, за всем следить надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do I have to keep an eye on everything?' Russian builds this impersonally with the dative ('to me' or 'to you') and 'it is necessary'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас есть полчаса, чтобы всё убрать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have half an hour to clean everything.' To express possession, Russian uses the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun followed by 'to remove'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Совсем крыша у него поехала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He has completely lost his mind.' Literally, 'his roof has completely slid off' — the classic Russian idiom for going crazy.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Надо ему срочно дать выпить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to urgently give him a drink.' Fedya links 'it is necessary' directly with 'him' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да как? Тут же камеры кругом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But how? There are cameras all around here.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'yes' directly with 'all around'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, кто там ещё пришёл?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, who else came there?' Chef Viktor links 'oh' directly with 'who' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, хотите выпить чайку?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, do you want to drink some tea?' Lyova links 'chef' directly with 'want' to express the thought clearly. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой к чёрту чай?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What the hell kind of tea?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'what a' directly with 'tea'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли вон, я вам сказал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out, I told you!' Chef Viktor links 'let's go' directly with 'over there' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, ну попробуйте этот чай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, please try this tea.' Lyova uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'try'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, вам обязательно понравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, you will definitely like it.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'chef' with 'will please'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что за инвалиды?",
      "gap": 0.5
